WEBVTT

NOTE week05-clip2-lora
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip draws its own caption along the bottom of the frame. These cues
NOTE sit there too, so that no part of the animation is ever covered.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.822
Full fine-tuning with the Adam optimizer
stores about 16 bytes for every parameter:

2
00:00:06.822 --> 00:00:10.631
the weight, its gradient,
and 2 optimizer states.

3
00:00:10.631 --> 00:00:14.462
That is about 5.8 gigabytes
for this small model,

4
00:00:14.462 --> 00:00:18.350
and over a thousand for a 70B model.

5
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.268
Look at one weight matrix in SmolLM2:

6
00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:27.758
a query projection with
960 rows and 960 columns,

7
00:00:27.758 --> 00:00:33.750
about 920,000 weights.
With LoRA, it stays frozen.

8
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.355
LoRA adds two thin matrices beside it.

9
00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:43.627
A takes the token down to just eight
numbers, and B brings it back up.

10
00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:48.617
The output is W times x,
plus B times A times x.

11
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:53.157
B and A together have 15,360 weights.

12
00:00:53.157 --> 00:00:59.617
That is about 1.7% of the frozen
matrix, and only these are trained.

13
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:05.065
B starts at zero, so at the first
step B times A times x is zero,

14
00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:12.683
and the model gives exactly the original
output. Training then moves only B and A.

15
00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:19.003
Add LoRA to the query, key, value, and
output projections in all 32 layers,

16
00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:23.622
and it trains 1,638,400 weights.

17
00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:28.417
That is about 0.45% of the model.

18
00:01:28.617 --> 00:01:33.689
Because the base model never changes,
one copy can serve many tasks.

19
00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:38.834
Each task, such as discharge
summaries or radiology impressions,

20
00:01:38.834 --> 00:01:44.885
needs only its own small
adapter, about 3.3 megabytes.
